insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('ci-f-1200','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What is the difference between an Access Control List (ACL) and a capability list?$$,$$An ACL is attached to an OBJECT and lists which subjects may access it and with what rights - "who can open this file." A capability list is attached to a SUBJECT and lists which objects that subject may access - "what can this user open." Both are two ways of representing the same underlying access control matrix, just read from opposite axes.$$),
('ci-f-1201','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What is Rule-Based Access Control (RuBAC), and how does it differ from Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) despite the similar name?$$,$$RuBAC grants or denies access by evaluating a set of IF-THEN rules against the current request (e.g., "deny all traffic after 6pm" or "block logins from outside the country"), independent of who the subject is. RBAC instead grants access based on the subject's assigned job role. The names are easy to confuse on exam questions, but RuBAC is condition-driven while RBAC is identity/role-driven.$$),
('ci-f-1202','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What is a constrained user interface, and what are two ways it can be implemented?$$,$$A constrained (restricted) user interface limits what a subject can even see or attempt, rather than granting broad access and relying on permission checks alone. Common implementations include menu-driven interfaces (only approved functions appear as options) and database views (only approved rows/columns are visible), which reduce the attack surface by removing the option to request unauthorized actions in the first place.$$),
('ci-f-1203','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What is content-dependent access control?$$,$$Access control where the decision depends on the actual DATA VALUES inside the object being requested, not just the subject's identity or role - for example, a customer-service rep's database query is automatically filtered to return only rows where the record's assigned region matches the rep's own region, rather than every row in the table.$$),
('ci-f-1204','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What is context-dependent access control?$$,$$Access control where the decision depends on the CIRCUMSTANCES surrounding the request rather than the content of the object itself - such as the time of day, the sequence of prior actions in a session, the requesting device's location, or whether a required prior step was already completed. ABAC engines commonly evaluate context-dependent attributes alongside subject and object attributes.$$),
('ci-f-1205','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What is the tradeoff between centralized and decentralized access control administration?$$,$$Centralized administration (a single team/system grants and revokes all access) gives consistent policy enforcement and an easy audit trail, but can bottleneck as an organization scales. Decentralized administration (local managers/data owners grant access within their own area) scales better and keeps decisions with people who know the local need, but risks inconsistent standards and makes org-wide access reviews harder.$$),
('ci-f-1206','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What does "default deny" (implicit deny) mean as an access control principle, and why is it preferred over "default allow"?$$,$$Default deny means any request not explicitly and affirmatively permitted is automatically rejected, rather than permitted unless specifically blocked. It is preferred because it fails safely: a missed or forgotten rule under default-deny results in an over-restrictive but SECURE outcome, while a missed rule under default-allow silently leaves a gap an attacker can walk through.$$),
('ci-f-1207','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$How does dual control (two-person integrity) differ from separation of duties?$$,$$Separation of duties splits a single END-TO-END PROCESS across multiple people so no one person can complete the whole thing alone (e.g., one person requests a payment, another approves it, on different days). Dual control requires two people to be PRESENT SIMULTANEOUSLY to complete one single sensitive action together (e.g., two safe-deposit keys, or two administrators each entering half a root encryption key). Dual control is about one action needing two people at once; SoD is about one workflow needing different people at different stages.$$),
('ci-f-1208','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$Why should administrators use a separate standard (non-privileged) account for everyday tasks like email and web browsing, rather than their privileged admin account?$$,$$If a phishing email or drive-by exploit compromises a session running under a privileged account, the attacker inherits that account's elevated rights immediately. Using a low-privilege account for routine, high-exposure activities and reserving the privileged account only for tasks that actually require elevation shrinks the window in which a routine compromise turns into a full administrative takeover - a direct application of least privilege to the accounts people use, not just the permissions those accounts hold.$$),
('ci-f-1209','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What does Privileged Access Management (PAM) tooling typically provide beyond simply granting an admin account elevated rights?$$,$$PAM systems vault privileged credentials so admins check them out rather than know them permanently, grant just-in-time (JIT) time-limited elevation instead of standing access, record and monitor privileged sessions, and automatically rotate credentials after use - shrinking both how long elevated access exists and how much of it goes unmonitored.$$),
('ci-a-400','cissp','acronym',5,'5.1',$$PAM$$,$$Privileged Access Management - vaults privileged credentials, grants just-in-time elevated access, and records/monitors privileged sessions rather than leaving standing admin rights in place.$$),
('ci-a-401','cissp','acronym',5,'5.1',$$ACL$$,$$Access Control List - a list attached to an OBJECT specifying which subjects may access it and with what rights; the object-centric counterpart to a subject-centric capability list.$$),
('ci-y-250','cissp','feynman',5,'5.1',$$Explain the difference between content-dependent and context-dependent access control, with an example of each.$$,$$Model answer: Both go beyond a simple "does this subject have permission on this object" check, but they look at different things. Content-dependent control inspects what is actually INSIDE the object - a bank teller querying an account database might have general read permission on the "accounts" table, but the query engine filters results so only rows matching accounts at the teller's own branch come back, based on a data value inside each row. Context-dependent control instead looks at the SITUATION surrounding the request, not the data itself - the same teller might be blocked from that same query entirely if they're logging in from an unrecognized country at 3am, or if they haven't yet completed a required identity-verification step earlier in the session. Content-dependent asks "what does this record say"; context-dependent asks "what's going on right now."$$),
('ci-y-251','cissp','feynman',5,'5.1',$$Explain why a bank vault might use dual control while a purchasing process uses separation of duties, and why swapping the two wouldn't work as well.$$,$$Model answer: A bank vault opening is a single, instantaneous action - the door either opens or it doesn't - so the only way to require two people's trust for that one action is to have both physically present at the same time, each supplying something the other lacks (two separate keys, or two half-combinations). That's dual control. A purchasing process, by contrast, isn't one instantaneous action - it's a sequence of steps (request, approval, payment) stretched over time, so fraud prevention there works by making sure no single person can perform every step alone, even at different times on different days. That's separation of duties. Using dual control for purchasing would be needlessly rigid, forcing two people to sit together through an entire multi-day workflow; using separation of duties for a vault wouldn't work at all, since there's no way to split a single "turn the handle" action into sequential stages performed by different people.$$),
('ci-f-1210','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$What is the difference between physiological and behavioral biometric factors?$$,$$Physiological biometrics measure a physical bodily characteristic - fingerprint ridges, iris/retina patterns, facial geometry, voice print. Behavioral biometrics instead measure a pattern in HOW someone does something - keystroke dynamics (typing rhythm), signature dynamics (pressure/speed while signing), or gait analysis (walking pattern). Both fall under "something you are," but behavioral biometrics can often be captured continuously and passively during normal use.$$),
('ci-f-1211','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$Why can't a compromised biometric credential simply be "reset" the way a leaked password can?$$,$$A password is an arbitrary secret that can be replaced with a new arbitrary secret. A fingerprint or iris pattern is a fixed physical characteristic a person has a limited number of - if the stored template or a high-fidelity copy of the trait itself is stolen, the person cannot generate a new fingerprint. This is why biometric systems store irreversible mathematical templates rather than raw images, and why revocation typically means switching to a different finger/eye or a different factor entirely, not "changing" the biometric.$$),
('ci-f-1212','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$What is liveness detection (presentation attack detection) in a biometric system?$$,$$A check that the biometric sample being read comes from a live, present subject rather than a spoof - a photo held up to a facial scanner, a lifted fingerprint on gelatin, or a recorded voice clip. Techniques include requiring blinking or head movement, measuring pulse/temperature, or challenge-response prompts, defeating "presentation attacks" that try to fool the sensor rather than defeat the underlying matching algorithm.$$),
('ci-f-1213','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$What is the difference between HOTP and TOTP one-time passwords?$$,$$HOTP (HMAC-based OTP) generates each code from a shared secret plus a COUNTER that increments every time a code is generated or used, so the code stays valid until it's actually consumed - useful for offline hardware tokens without a clock. TOTP (Time-based OTP) instead derives the code from the shared secret plus the CURRENT TIME, typically changing every 30 seconds, which requires the token and server's clocks to stay reasonably synchronized but doesn't depend on tracking usage counts.$$),
('ci-f-1214','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$What is a push-bombing (MFA fatigue) attack?$$,$$An attacker who already has a victim's password repeatedly triggers push-based MFA approval prompts on the victim's phone, hoping the victim eventually taps "approve" out of annoyance, confusion, or to make the notifications stop - defeating MFA not by breaking the cryptography but by exploiting user fatigue. Mitigations include number-matching challenges, rate-limiting prompts, and switching to phishing-resistant methods like FIDO2 security keys.$$),
('ci-f-1215','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$What does NIST SP 800-63B's modern password guidance change from older practices?$$,$$It favors long, memorable passphrases over short complex strings with mandatory symbol/number rules, recommends AGAINST periodic forced password rotation absent evidence of compromise (since forced rotation drives predictable patterns like "Summer2024!" to "Summer2025!"), and requires screening new passwords against known-breached-password lists rather than relying on complexity rules alone.$$),
('ci-a-402','cissp','acronym',5,'5.2',$$TOTP$$,$$Time-based One-Time Password - an OTP algorithm that derives each code from a shared secret and the current time (typically a 30-second window), requiring synchronized clocks between token and server.$$),
('ci-a-403','cissp','acronym',5,'5.2',$$FIDO2$$,$$Fast IDentity Online 2 - a passwordless authentication standard using public-key cryptography (via WebAuthn) so the private key never leaves the user's device/security key, making it resistant to phishing and credential-database breaches alike.$$),
('ci-a-404','cissp','acronym',5,'5.2',$$MFA$$,$$Multi-Factor Authentication - authentication requiring two or more DIFFERENT factor categories (something you know/have/are) rather than two secrets from the same category.$$),
('ci-y-252','cissp','feynman',5,'5.2',$$Explain why a leaked fingerprint template is a fundamentally different kind of problem than a leaked password database, and what design choice biometric systems make because of it.$$,$$Model answer: A password database breach is bad, but recoverable - every affected user changes their password to a brand-new secret, and the old leaked value becomes worthless. A biometric trait can't be swapped out the same way: a person has exactly two irises, ten fingerprints, and one face, and those don't change on demand. If an attacker obtains a high-fidelity copy of the underlying physical trait itself, that specific factor is compromised indefinitely for that person, everywhere it's used. Because of this, well-designed biometric systems never store the raw scan; they store a one-way mathematical template derived from it, similar in spirit to a password hash, so that even a database breach doesn't hand the attacker a usable copy of the original fingerprint or face - the comparison happens against the template, not a photograph the attacker could replay elsewhere.$$),
('ci-f-1216','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is Federated Identity Management (FIM), and what problem does it solve across organizational boundaries?$$,$$FIM lets a user authenticate once with their home organization (the identity provider) and use that single identity to access resources at OTHER, separately administered organizations (relying parties) that trust the home organization's assertions - without each relying party needing to create and manage a separate account and password for that user. It extends SSO's convenience across trust boundaries that a single company's internal directory alone cannot cross.$$),
('ci-f-1217','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is a "circle of trust" in federated identity, and what has to be established before it works?$$,$$A circle of trust is the set of organizations (one or more identity providers and one or more relying/service providers) that have agreed, in advance, to trust each other's assertions about identity. Before federation works, the parties must exchange metadata, agree on which attributes will be shared, establish cryptographic trust (signing certificates), and define the legal/contractual terms for what each party is liable for if an assertion turns out to be wrong.$$),
('ci-f-1218','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What does a cloud Identity Provider (IdP) such as Azure AD/Entra ID, Okta, or Google Workspace provide as a third-party identity service?$$,$$A cloud IdP centralizes identity storage, authentication, and policy enforcement (MFA, conditional access, password policy) as a hosted service that an organization's own applications, and often many external SaaS applications, can all trust via standard federation protocols - letting the organization outsource identity infrastructure operation rather than run its own directory servers for every integration.$$),
('ci-f-1219','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is the difference between on-premises, cloud, and hybrid identity architecture?$$,$$On-premises identity keeps the directory (e.g., Active Directory) and authentication entirely inside the organization's own data center. Cloud identity moves the directory to a provider-hosted service with no on-prem directory at all. Hybrid identity keeps an on-premises directory as the authoritative source but synchronizes or federates it with a cloud identity service, so users authenticate consistently against both on-prem and cloud/SaaS resources without maintaining two separate identities.$$),
('ci-f-1220','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$In a hybrid identity setup, what is the difference between password hash synchronization and pass-through authentication?$$,$$Password hash sync copies a hash of each user's on-premises password up to the cloud directory, so the cloud service can validate logins independently even if the on-prem network is unreachable. Pass-through authentication instead keeps password validation happening entirely on-premises - the cloud login prompt forwards the credential check back to an on-prem agent in real time, so no password hash is ever stored in the cloud, at the cost of requiring on-prem infrastructure to be available for every cloud login.$$),
('ci-f-1221','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What does SCIM (System for Cross-domain Identity Management) automate in a federated identity relationship?$$,$$SCIM standardizes how user and group account data is automatically created, updated, and deactivated across separately administered systems - so when an employee is added, changes department, or is terminated in the authoritative HR/identity source, connected third-party applications receive that provisioning/deprovisioning change automatically via a common API, instead of relying on a manual or delayed process at each downstream app.$$),
('ci-f-1222','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is an identity broker, and why might an organization use one instead of federating directly with each partner?$$,$$An identity broker is an intermediary service that sits between multiple identity providers and multiple relying parties, translating between different federation protocols or identity formats (e.g., accepting a SAML assertion from one partner and issuing an OIDC token to an application that only understands OIDC). Rather than building and maintaining a direct, protocol-matched trust relationship with every partner individually, an organization federates once with the broker, which handles the protocol translation and multiplies the number of usable trust relationships.$$),
('ci-f-1223','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is just-in-time (JIT) provisioning in a federated identity flow?$$,$$Instead of pre-creating a local account for every user who might ever federate in, JIT provisioning automatically creates (or updates) the user's local account record the FIRST TIME they successfully authenticate via the trusted identity provider, populating it from attributes carried in that first assertion/token - reducing administrative overhead for large partner populations where most users may never actually log in.$$),
('ci-f-1224','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is the difference between B2B and B2C federated identity scenarios?$$,$$B2B (business-to-business) federation lets employees of a PARTNER ORGANIZATION access your systems using their own employer's identity, common for supply-chain and contractor access. B2C (business-to-consumer) federation instead lets individual CONSUMERS log in using an identity from a large public provider they already have, such as "Sign in with Google," so the business avoids managing separate consumer passwords. Both reuse someone else's identity assertion, but the trusted party is an organization in one case and a mass-market consumer identity provider in the other.$$),
('ci-a-405','cissp','acronym',5,'5.3',$$FIM$$,$$Federated Identity Management - lets a user authenticate once with a home identity provider and use that identity at separately administered relying parties that trust its assertions, without a separate account at each one.$$),
('ci-a-406','cissp','acronym',5,'5.3',$$SCIM$$,$$System for Cross-domain Identity Management - a standard API for automatically provisioning, updating, and deprovisioning user/group accounts across separately administered systems in a federated relationship.$$),
('ci-a-407','cissp','acronym',5,'5.3',$$IdP$$,$$Identity Provider - the party in a federated or SSO relationship that authenticates the user and issues a signed assertion or token, which relying/service providers trust instead of independently verifying the user's credentials.$$),
('ci-y-253','cissp','feynman',5,'5.3',$$Explain how hybrid identity lets an employee log into both an on-premises file server and a cloud SaaS app with one set of credentials, using either password hash sync or federation.$$,$$Model answer: The organization keeps its on-premises directory (say, Active Directory) as the single source of truth for who the employee is and what their password should be - that directory already handles the on-prem file server login the old-fashioned way. The trick is extending that same identity to the cloud without creating a second, separately managed account there. One approach is password hash synchronization: a background sync tool periodically copies a cryptographic hash of the on-prem password up to the cloud identity service, so when the employee logs into the SaaS app, the cloud service checks the attempt against that synced hash itself, entirely independently of the on-prem network being reachable at that moment. The other approach is federation: instead of copying any password material to the cloud at all, the cloud login page redirects the employee back to an on-premises authentication service, which checks the password locally and sends back a signed assertion saying "this user is who they claim to be," which the cloud app trusts without ever seeing or storing the password. Both approaches produce the same experience - one password, working everywhere - but they differ in whether a copy of password material ever leaves the on-premises environment.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('ci-q-1200','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',1,$q$A system stores, for each user account, its own list of every file that account is permitted to open. This list travels with the user account rather than being attached to the files themselves. What access control structure does this describe?$q$,$q$["A capability list","An Access Control List (ACL)","A rule base","A security clearance label"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A capability list is subject-centric: it is attached to the subject and enumerates the objects that subject may access. An ACL is the opposite, object-centric structure - attached to the object, listing which subjects may access it. A rule base evaluates conditions rather than listing objects/subjects directly, and a clearance label is a MAC concept, not a list structure.$q$),
('ci-q-1201','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$A firewall blocks any connection attempt made outside 8am-6pm local time and denies any traffic sourced from a specific country's address blocks, applying these same evaluated conditions no matter which account or process originated the traffic. Which access control model is this?$q$,$q$["Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)", "Rule-Based Access Control (RuBAC)", "Discretionary Access Control (DAC)", "Mandatory Access Control (MAC)"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$RuBAC evaluates IF-THEN conditions (time window, source address) against each request, independent of who or what role is making it - easily confused with RBAC by name, but RBAC instead grants access based on the subject's assigned job role, not a condition evaluated against the request itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1202','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',1,$q$A bank's ATM presents only "Withdraw," "Deposit," and "Check Balance" as selectable options on screen - there is no way for a customer to even attempt to view another customer's account, because that option was never presented in the first place. What access control technique does this describe?$q$,$q$["Attribute-Based Access Control", "Mandatory Access Control", "A constrained (restricted) user interface", "Content-dependent access control"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$A constrained user interface limits what a subject can even attempt by removing unauthorized options from the presented menu entirely, rather than presenting every option and relying solely on a permission check to reject unauthorized attempts after the fact.$q$),
('ci-q-1203','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$A sales representative runs the same customer-database query as every other representative, but the application automatically filters the results so that only rows whose stored "assigned region" field matches the representative's own region are ever returned. Which access control concept does this filtering represent?$q$,$q$["Context-dependent access control", "Role-Based Access Control", "Rule-Based Access Control", "Content-dependent access control"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$The filtering decision depends on a data VALUE stored inside each row (the region field) - the defining trait of content-dependent access control. Context-dependent control would instead depend on circumstances surrounding the request, such as time or location, not values inside the object itself.$q$),
('ci-q-1204','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$A user's session is blocked from completing a sensitive transaction because the login originated from an unrecognized country at 3am local time, even though the user's role and the requested data itself are entirely ordinary. Which access control concept does this illustrate?$q$,$q$["Context-dependent access control","Content-dependent access control","A capability list","A constrained user interface"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The block is driven by circumstances surrounding the request - the time and location - rather than anything inside the requested data itself, which is the defining trait of context-dependent access control, distinct from content-dependent control's focus on data values within the object.$q$),
('ci-q-1205','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$A multinational company routes every access request through one small central team to guarantee identical, auditable policy enforcement worldwide. As the company grows, employees increasingly wait weeks for routine access approvals. What administration-model tradeoff does this scenario illustrate?$q$,$q$["Decentralized administration trading consistency for scalability", "Centralized administration trading scalability for policy consistency", "Rule-Based Access Control replacing Role-Based Access Control", "Mandatory Access Control replacing Discretionary Access Control"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Centralized access administration keeps enforcement consistent and easy to audit through a single authority, but that same single chokepoint becomes a scalability bottleneck as request volume grows - the classic tradeoff against decentralized administration, which scales better but risks inconsistent local decisions.$q$),
('ci-q-1206','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',1,$q$A firewall administrator forgets to write a rule for a newly deployed application's traffic. Because any traffic that matches no explicit rule falls through to an implicit deny at the bottom of the rule base, the new application simply fails to connect rather than being silently exposed. What principle produced this fail-safe outcome?$q$,$q$["Default allow", "Separation of duties", "Default deny (implicit deny)", "Need to know"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Default deny means anything not explicitly, affirmatively permitted is rejected - so a missed rule fails toward an over-restrictive but SECURE outcome. Under default allow, the same missed rule would instead have silently left a gap an attacker could exploit.$q$),
('ci-q-1207','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$Opening a bank's vault requires two specific managers to insert their separately held keys into the same lock at the same time; neither manager's key alone can open it. Which access control concept does this describe?$q$,$q$["Separation of duties", "Need to know", "Role-Based Access Control", "Dual control (two-person integrity)"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Dual control requires two people to be present and act together to complete one single sensitive action. Separation of duties instead splits a multi-step PROCESS across different people at different stages, so no one person completes the whole workflow alone - a related but distinct concept from requiring simultaneous joint action on one step.$q$),
('ci-q-1208','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',3,$q$A security architect requires every system administrator to hold two separate accounts: a low-privilege account used for email and everyday browsing, and a separate privileged account invoked only for administrative tasks and never used to check email. What is the PRIMARY security purpose of this separation?$q$,$q$["Limiting the blast radius if the low-privilege account or session is compromised via phishing or a drive-by exploit","Reducing the total number of accounts the help desk must manage","Satisfying a password-complexity requirement","Enabling single sign-on between the two accounts"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$If a routine phishing email or exploit compromises a session running under the low-privilege account, the attacker inherits only that account's limited rights rather than immediately gaining administrative control - a direct application of least privilege to which account is used for which activity, not just to what permissions an account holds.$q$),
('ci-q-1209','cissp',5,'5.1','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements below that correctly describe an access control matrix and the structures commonly derived from it.$q$,$q$["Rows can represent subjects and columns can represent the objects those subjects may access","A capability list corresponds to one subject's row extracted from the matrix","An Access Control List (ACL) corresponds to one subject's row extracted from the matrix","The access control matrix concept only applies to Mandatory Access Control systems"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$An access control matrix maps subjects (rows) against objects (columns), with each cell holding the permitted rights. A capability list is exactly one subject's row, listing what that subject may access. An ACL, by contrast, is one OBJECT's column, listing which subjects may access it - not a subject's row. The matrix concept applies broadly across access control models, not only MAC.$q$),
('ci-q-1210','cissp',5,'5.1','ordering',3,$q$Order the steps of a typical just-in-time (JIT) privileged access workflow managed through a PAM system, from first to last.$q$,$q$["An administrator submits a request for temporary elevated access to a specific system","An approver reviews and authorizes the request","The privileged credential is checked out from the vault for a limited time window","The administrator's privileged session is recorded while elevated access is active","The credential is automatically rotated and checked back into the vault when the time window ends"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$A PAM-managed JIT workflow begins with a request, requires approval before any credential is released, checks the credential out of the vault only for a bounded window, records the session while elevated rights are active, and finally rotates and returns the credential automatically once the window closes - minimizing how long, and how visibly, elevated access exists.$q$),
('ci-q-1211','cissp',5,'5.1','matching',2,$q$Match each access control concept on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Access Control List (ACL)","Capability list","Rule-Based Access Control (RuBAC)","Constrained user interface"],"right":["Restricts a subject's presented options, such as menu items or visible database rows, rather than relying on permission checks alone","Attached to an object, listing which subjects may access it and with what rights","Evaluates IF-THEN conditions against a request, independent of the requester's identity or role","Attached to a subject, listing which objects that subject may access"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$An ACL is object-centric (attached to the object). A capability list is subject-centric (attached to the subject). RuBAC evaluates conditions against the request itself, regardless of identity. A constrained user interface removes unauthorized options from what is even presented, rather than presenting everything and checking permissions after the fact.$q$),
('ci-q-1212','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',1,$q$An authentication system continuously analyzes the rhythm and timing of a user's typing throughout an active session to confirm the same person is still at the keyboard, without requiring any additional scan or prompt. What type of biometric factor is this?$q$,$q$["A physiological biometric (fingerprint)", "A behavioral biometric (keystroke dynamics)", "A knowledge factor", "A possession factor"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Keystroke dynamics measures a pattern in HOW someone performs an action (typing rhythm), which is the defining trait of a behavioral biometric, distinct from a physiological biometric that measures a fixed physical characteristic like a fingerprint. Neither a knowledge nor possession factor describes a biometric measurement at all.$q$),
('ci-q-1213','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$An attacker holds a high-resolution printed photo of an authorized employee up to a facial-recognition camera. The system prompts "please blink twice" before it will attempt a match, and rejects the photo when no blink occurs. What capability is the system using?$q$,$q$["False Acceptance Rate tuning", "Behavioral biometric enrollment", "Liveness detection (presentation attack detection)", "Key stretching"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Liveness detection specifically checks that the sample comes from a live, present subject rather than a spoof like a photo, recording, or mask - defeating this "presentation attack" through a challenge such as requiring a blink, independent of how the underlying matching algorithm's FAR/FRR is tuned.$q$),
('ci-q-1214','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',1,$q$A hardware token has no internal clock. Each time its button is pressed, it generates a new one-time code derived from a shared secret and an internal counter that increments with each use, and the server tracks the same counter to stay synchronized. Which one-time-password standard does this describe?$q$,$q$["TOTP (Time-based One-Time Password)", "FIDO2", "OpenID Connect", "HOTP (HMAC-based One-Time Password)"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$HOTP derives each code from a shared secret plus an incrementing counter, requiring no clock at all - well suited to a simple offline token. TOTP instead derives codes from the shared secret plus the current time, which requires clock synchronization between token and server rather than counter tracking.$q$),
('ci-q-1215','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',3,$q$An attacker who already possesses a victim's stolen password repeatedly triggers push-based MFA approval prompts on the victim's phone at 2am, hoping the victim eventually taps "Approve" simply to stop the notifications. What is this attack called, and what is an effective mitigation?$q$,$q$["Push bombing (MFA fatigue); mitigated by requiring number-matching on each approval rather than a single tap","Pass-the-hash; mitigated by disabling NTLM authentication","A rainbow table attack; mitigated by salting stored password hashes","Kerberoasting; mitigated by using long service account passwords"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Push bombing exploits user fatigue rather than breaking MFA's underlying cryptography - flooding the victim with approval prompts until one is accidentally or wearily approved. Requiring the user to match a displayed number on the login screen to the number shown in the push prompt (number matching) defeats a blind "just tap approve" response. The other three attacks and mitigations are real but describe different techniques entirely.$q$),
('ci-q-1216','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',1,$q$Security logs show a single password, "Winter2026!", attempted exactly once against each of 10,000 different corporate accounts over several hours, never repeating an account and never exceeding any individual account's lockout threshold. What is this attack called?$q$,$q$["Credential stuffing", "Password spraying", "A brute-force attack against one account", "Pass-the-hash"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Password spraying tries one (or a few) common password against MANY different accounts, deliberately staying under each account's lockout threshold to avoid detection - the opposite shape of brute force, which hammers many guesses against a single account, and distinct from credential stuffing, which replays previously breached username/password PAIRS rather than guessing a fresh password.$q$),
('ci-q-1217','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$An attacker steals a database of unsalted password hashes and, entirely offline with no login attempts against the live system, instantly recovers many plaintext passwords by looking each hash up in a precomputed table of hash-to-plaintext mappings. Which single defense, applied before the breach, would have made that precomputed table useless?$q$,$q$["Requiring MFA on every login", "Enforcing an account lockout threshold", "Adding a unique random salt to each password before hashing", "Encrypting the network connection with TLS"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$A rainbow table is only useful against hashes it was precomputed for; a unique random salt per password means each password's effective hash input differs even for identical passwords, so no generic precomputed table can match it, forcing the attacker back to computing each password individually. MFA, lockouts, and TLS all protect the live login process, but this attack never touched the live login process at all - it worked entirely offline against the stolen hash database.$q$),
('ci-q-1218','cissp',5,'5.2','multi',2,$q$Select the TWO statements below that correctly describe FIDO2/WebAuthn passwordless authentication.$q$,$q$["The private key is generated and stored on the user's device or security key and is never transmitted to the server","Authentication is resistant to phishing because the credential is cryptographically bound to the legitimate site's origin","It requires the user to memorize and periodically rotate a long passphrase","It relies on a secret value stored identically on both the client and the server"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$FIDO2/WebAuthn uses public-key cryptography: the private key stays on the user's device/security key and never travels to the server, and the credential is bound to the requesting site's origin so a phishing site presenting a fake domain cannot obtain a usable signature. There is no passphrase to memorize or rotate, and unlike a shared secret (such as a password hash), there is no symmetric secret value stored identically on both ends.$q$),
('ci-q-1219','cissp',5,'5.2','ordering',3,$q$Order the steps of an adaptive (risk-based) authentication decision, from first to last.$q$,$q$["The system collects contextual signals about the login attempt, such as device, location, time, and network reputation","The system calculates a risk score by comparing the collected signals against the user's normal behavior baseline","If the risk score exceeds a defined threshold, the system prompts for an additional authentication step (step-up MFA)","If the risk score stays low, the system grants access using only the originally supplied factor"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Adaptive authentication first gathers contextual signals about the attempt, then scores the risk those signals represent against the user's established baseline, and only THEN decides whether to demand additional proof (step-up) or allow the original factor to stand - context collection and scoring must happen before any step-up decision can be made.$q$),
('ci-q-1220','cissp',5,'5.2','matching',2,$q$Match each credential or authentication attack on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Password spraying","Rainbow table attack","Push bombing (MFA fatigue)","Liveness spoofing"],"right":["Repeatedly sends MFA approval prompts, hoping the victim eventually accepts one out of annoyance","Tries one common password across many accounts to stay under each account's lockout threshold","Presents a photo, recording, or mask to a biometric sensor to impersonate a live subject","Looks up a stolen password hash against a precomputed table of hash-to-plaintext mappings, entirely offline"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Password spraying spreads one guess across many accounts; a rainbow table attack works entirely offline against stolen hashes using precomputation; push bombing exploits approval fatigue rather than breaking any cryptography; liveness spoofing tries to fool a biometric sensor's presentation-attack detection with a fake physical sample rather than attacking a password or hash at all.$q$),
('ci-q-1221','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',1,$q$A contractor from a partner firm logs into your company's extranet portal using credentials that are issued and managed entirely by their own employer, and your company never stores or even sees that contractor's password. What does this scenario describe?$q$,$q$["Local account provisioning", "Discretionary Access Control", "Content-dependent access control", "Federated Identity Management (FIM)"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$FIM lets a user authenticate with their own home organization's identity provider and use that identity at a separately administered relying party that trusts the assertion - the relying party never needs to create or manage a local password for that user at all.$q$),
('ci-q-1222','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$Before two organizations' identity systems can federate, they exchange signing certificates and metadata, agree on which user attributes will be shared, and define contractually who bears liability if an assertion later turns out to be false. What is this pre-established relationship called?$q$,$q$["A circle of trust","A constrained user interface","Content-dependent access control","Key stretching"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A circle of trust is the set of identity providers and relying parties that have agreed in advance to trust each other's identity assertions, underpinned by exchanged cryptographic trust material and contractual terms - federation cannot function safely without this groundwork being established first.$q$),
('ci-q-1223','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',1,$q$A company stops operating its own directory servers for every new SaaS integration and instead centralizes authentication, MFA enforcement, and conditional-access policy in a single hosted identity service that dozens of internal and external applications all trust. What is this hosted service functioning as?$q$,$q$["A capability list", "A cloud Identity Provider (IdP)", "A rule base", "A constrained user interface"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A cloud IdP (such as Azure AD/Entra ID, Okta, or Google Workspace) centralizes identity storage, authentication, and policy enforcement as a hosted, third-party-operated service, letting an organization outsource identity infrastructure rather than run separate directory integrations per application.$q$),
('ci-q-1224','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$An organization keeps its on-premises Active Directory as the authoritative source of user identity, but synchronizes those accounts to a cloud identity service so employees can log into both local file shares and cloud SaaS applications using one identity. What kind of identity architecture is this?$q$,$q$["Pure cloud identity architecture", "Pure on-premises identity architecture", "Hybrid identity architecture", "B2C federated identity"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Hybrid identity keeps an on-premises directory authoritative while synchronizing or federating it with a cloud identity service, so users authenticate consistently across both environments. Pure cloud identity would have no on-prem directory at all, and pure on-premises identity would have no cloud synchronization or federation in place.$q$),
('ci-q-1225','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$An organization requires that no copy of any password hash ever leave its on-premises network, even in synchronized form, so every cloud application login must be validated in real time by an on-premises authentication agent. Which hybrid-identity method satisfies this requirement?$q$,$q$["Password hash synchronization", "Just-in-time provisioning", "SCIM provisioning", "Pass-through authentication"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Pass-through authentication forwards each cloud login attempt back to an on-premises agent for real-time password validation, so no password hash is ever stored in the cloud. Password hash synchronization, by contrast, does copy a hash of the password to the cloud so it can validate logins independently - the opposite of what this requirement demands.$q$),
('ci-q-1226','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$When HR marks an employee as terminated in the authoritative HR system, dozens of connected third-party SaaS applications automatically disable that employee's account within minutes through a standard provisioning API, with no manual step required at any individual application. What technology enables this?$q$,$q$["SCIM (System for Cross-domain Identity Management)","Content-dependent access control","Rule-Based Access Control","Dual control"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SCIM standardizes automated account provisioning and deprovisioning across separately administered systems via a common API, so a change at the authoritative source propagates automatically to every connected application rather than relying on a manual or delayed process at each one.$q$),
('ci-q-1227','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',3,$q$A hub service accepts a signed assertion from Partner A's identity provider using one federation protocol, and reissues an equivalent token using a different federation protocol for an internal application that only understands that second protocol, so the application never needs to speak the partner's original protocol directly. What is this hub service called?$q$,$q$["A cloud access security broker", "An identity broker", "A capability list", "A constrained user interface"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An identity broker sits between multiple identity providers and relying parties, translating between different federation protocols or token formats, so an organization can federate once with the broker rather than building a separate, protocol-matched trust relationship with every partner individually.$q$),
('ci-q-1228','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$A partner organization's employee logs into your portal for the very first time using their federated identity. Rather than requiring any pre-registration, your system automatically creates a local account record for them, populated from attributes carried in that first successful authentication. What is this called?$q$,$q$["Password hash synchronization", "Dual control", "Just-in-time (JIT) provisioning", "Rule-Based Access Control"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$JIT provisioning creates or updates a user's local account automatically at the moment of their first successful federated authentication, rather than requiring every possible user to be pre-created in advance - useful when only a fraction of a large partner population will ever actually log in.$q$),
('ci-q-1229','cissp',5,'5.3','multi',3,$q$Select the TWO statements below that correctly describe a genuine risk introduced by relying on federated identity across many relying parties.$q$,$q$["A compromise of the identity provider can grant an attacker access to every relying party that trusts its assertions","Federation eliminates the need for any relying party to enforce its own authorization decisions","Adding each new relying party expands the pool of resources reachable through a single compromised identity","Federation guarantees that every connected relying party enforces identical local password policy"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Because many relying parties trust the same identity provider, a compromise at that one IdP can cascade into access at every relying party trusting it, and each additional relying party added to the federation further widens what a single compromised identity can reach. Relying parties still make their own authorization decisions after trusting an assertion, and federation says nothing about relying parties' own LOCAL policies, which can still differ.$q$),
('ci-q-1230','cissp',5,'5.3','ordering',2,$q$Order the steps of a generic federated authentication flow across an organizational boundary, from first to last.$q$,$q$["The user requests a protected resource at the relying party (service provider)","The relying party redirects the user to the user's home identity provider for authentication","The user authenticates directly with their home identity provider","The identity provider issues a signed assertion or token back to the relying party","The relying party validates the trust relationship and signature, then grants access based on the assertion"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Federation begins with a resource request at the relying party, which redirects the user to authenticate at their own home identity provider rather than handling authentication itself; only after the identity provider issues a signed assertion back does the relying party validate the trust relationship and signature before granting access - the relying party never handles the user's actual credentials at any point.$q$),
('ci-q-1231','cissp',5,'5.3','matching',2,$q$Match each hybrid or federated identity concept on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Password hash synchronization","Pass-through authentication","Just-in-time (JIT) provisioning","B2B federation"],"right":["Automatically creates a local account the first time a federated user successfully authenticates","Validates a login by forwarding the check to an on-premises agent in real time, storing no password hash in the cloud","Copies a hash of each on-premises password to the cloud directory so it can validate logins independently","Lets employees of a partner organization access your systems using their own employer's identity"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,1,0,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Password hash sync copies hashed password material to the cloud for independent validation; pass-through authentication instead validates every login on-premises in real time with nothing copied to the cloud; JIT provisioning creates the local account at first federated login; B2B federation is the specific scenario of trusting a partner organization's own employee identities rather than a mass-market consumer identity provider.$q$);
